{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"out='/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/graphics/runs/run-20260426T210247Z--1zoxvhk500/semantic/comment_section_generation/topic--topic-real-time-graphics-as-performance-medium.json'\nmkdir -p \\\"\"'$(dirname \"$out\")\"\ncat > \"$out\" <<'\"'JSON'\n{\n  \\\"page_ref\\\": \\\"topic:topic-real-time-graphics-as-performance-medium\\\",\n  \\\"requested_count\\\": 5,\n  \\\"comments\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"comment_ref\\\": \\\"draft-1\\\",\n      \\\"persona_id\\\": \\\"persona-maya-santoro\\\",\n      \\\"body\\\": \\\"I appreciate that this page does not flatten performance into a single meaning. Shadercoding is performative because the audience can watch code become image under time pressure, while the crowd-rendering paper treats performance as an engineering threshold where a city still feels alive at interactive frame rates.\\\",\n      \\\"parent_ref\\\": null,\n      \\\"score_assessment\\\": {\n        \\\"score\\\": 24,\n        \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Strongly grounded in both sources and clearly preserves the artistic versus engineering distinction.\\\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"comment_ref\\\": \\\"draft-2\\\",\n      \\\"persona_id\\\": \\\"persona-eli-okafor\\\",\n      \\\"body\\\": \\\"The comparison works best when real time is treated as a constraint that changes the medium. In the shadercoding case, formulas and fragment shaders become visible improvisational tools; in the crowd paper, impostors, compression, and recoloring are valuable because they keep thousands of animated people within the frame budget.\\\",\n      \\\"parent_ref\\\": null,\n      \\\"score_assessment\\\": {\n        \\\"score\\\": 23,\n        \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Accurately connects technical mechanisms from both sources to the shared live-constraint theme.\\\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"comment_ref\\\": \\\"draft-3\\\",\n      \\\"persona_id\\\": \\\"persona-lina-kovac\\\",\n      \\\"body\\\": \\\"I would be careful not to imply that the crowd-rendering work is performance art in the same sense as Shader Showdowns. Its performance claim is mostly about measured interactivity in a virtual city, not about spectators watching the renderer being made.\\\",\n      \\\"parent_ref\\\": \\\"draft-1\\\",\n      \\\"score_assessment\\\": {\n        \\\"score\\\": 25,\n        \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Useful threaded correction that reinforces the page's stated domain boundary without dismissing the comparison.\\\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"comment_ref\\\": \\\"draft-4\\\",\n      \\\"persona_id\\\": \\\"persona-rory-chen\\\",\n      \\\"body\\\": \\\"What links the two examples for me is technical control under scarcity. A live shader coder has minutes, GPU rules, and mathematical notation; the crowd renderer has texture memory, popping artifacts, and frame time. In both cases, the visual result depends on making constraints productive rather than pretending they are absent.\\\",\n      \\\"parent_ref\\\": null,\n      \\\"score_assessment\\\": {\n        \\\"score\\\": 22,\n        \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Well written synthesis with concrete constraints, though it is more interpretive than evidentiary.\\\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"comment_ref\\\": \\\"draft-5\\\",\n      \\\"persona_id\\\": \\\"persona-samira-haddad\\\",\n      \\\"body\\\": \\\"The evidence balance is worth noting. The shadercoding source is strongest as reflective practice and theory around live coding, while the crowd-rendering source has clearer benchmark evidence for scale. Reading them together is productive, but their claims should not be scored by the same kind of proof.\\\",\n      \\\"parent_ref\\\": null,\n      \\\"score_assessment\\\": {\n        \\\"score\\\": 26,\n        \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Excellent source-aware comment that distinguishes evidence types and improves critical interpretation of the topic.\\\"\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\nJSON\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
